1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,407
Every observability tool tells you what
happened.

2
00:00:02,407 --> 00:00:04,371
None of them tell you whether it worked.

3
00:00:04,371 --> 00:00:08,202
Here is why that matters, and what a tool
built around it actually looks like.

4
00:00:09,802 --> 00:00:13,544
Start with one order, from a checkout
platform of five services.

5
00:00:13,544 --> 00:00:15,240
This is everything it logged.

6
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,117
Order received.

7
00:00:16,117 --> 00:00:16,994
Cart validated.

8
00:00:16,994 --> 00:00:18,105
Catalogue searched.

9
00:00:18,105 --> 00:00:21,789
Payment authorised, seventeen thousand
four hundred and ninety.

10
00:00:21,789 --> 00:00:22,549
Email queued.

11
00:00:22,549 --> 00:00:27,168
Order completed, status two hundred, four
hundred and ninety five milliseconds.

12
00:00:27,168 --> 00:00:27,753
No errors.

13
00:00:27,753 --> 00:00:28,045
Fast.

14
00:00:28,045 --> 00:00:29,741
Card charged, customer happy.

15
00:00:29,741 --> 00:00:34,009
Every dashboard you own calls this green,
and every one of them is wrong.

16
00:00:36,209 --> 00:00:38,152
The failure is not in those lines.

17
00:00:38,152 --> 00:00:40,096
It is in a line that is not there.

18
00:00:40,096 --> 00:00:43,583
A healthy order logs this: inventory
service, stock reserved.

19
00:00:43,583 --> 00:00:46,441
Order eight eight four zero nine has no
such line.

20
00:00:46,441 --> 00:00:47,584
Inventory never ran.

21
00:00:47,584 --> 00:00:49,298
So the money left the account,

22
00:00:49,298 --> 00:00:53,471
the confirmation email went out, and no
warehouse knows to ship anything.

23
00:00:53,471 --> 00:00:56,043
Nothing to alert on, because nothing
errored.

24
00:00:56,043 --> 00:00:57,072
The run completed.

25
00:00:57,072 --> 00:00:58,158
Status two hundred.

26
00:00:58,158 --> 00:01:01,245
The only thing that failed was the point
of the order,

27
00:01:01,245 --> 00:01:04,903
and no tool out there has an opinion about
what an order is for.

28
00:01:07,303 --> 00:01:09,167
Aegis gives that a name: hollow.

29
00:01:09,167 --> 00:01:11,265
Completed cleanly, achieved nothing.

30
00:01:11,265 --> 00:01:12,954
Name it and you can count it,

31
00:01:12,954 --> 00:01:16,741
and in this one file it happens three
times in twenty six orders.

32
00:01:16,741 --> 00:01:18,023
Compare the two tools.

33
00:01:18,023 --> 00:01:20,411
An error based tool reports two failures,

34
00:01:20,411 --> 00:01:23,907
and it is right about both, those are the
declined payments.

35
00:01:23,907 --> 00:01:28,393
It says nothing about the three orders
that took money and delivered nothing.

36
00:01:28,393 --> 00:01:29,791
That gap is the product.

37
00:01:32,191 --> 00:01:33,066
So, six stages.

38
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:35,748
It watches your logs and your code, read
only.

39
00:01:35,748 --> 00:01:38,896
It notices what matters using statistics,
not a model.

40
00:01:38,896 --> 00:01:41,345
It judges every run, hollow ones included.

41
00:01:41,345 --> 00:01:43,794
It explains, with the lines that prove it.

42
00:01:43,794 --> 00:01:46,418
It fixes, with a test that had to fail
first.

43
00:01:46,418 --> 00:01:49,333
And then it checks whether that fix
actually held.

44
00:01:49,333 --> 00:01:53,823
Watch for that last one, because every
other tool in this space stops at fix.

45
00:01:56,023 --> 00:01:59,141
This is Aegis, pointed at that platform
with one command.

46
00:01:59,141 --> 00:02:00,509
Down the left, the pages.

47
00:02:00,509 --> 00:02:02,807
Across the top, the tabs that do the work.

48
00:02:02,807 --> 00:02:04,284
In the middle a live brief,

49
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:11,341
written as the stream moves, and you can
switch it off, because it is the one thing
here that is prose rather than a
measurement.

50
00:02:11,341 --> 00:02:12,545
Below that the funnel,

51
00:02:12,545 --> 00:02:16,593
then run quality: twenty achieved, three
hollow, two failed, one degraded.

52
00:02:16,593 --> 00:02:18,508
Every number got there by counting.

53
00:02:20,508 --> 00:02:21,089
Stage one.

54
00:02:21,089 --> 00:02:23,880
No schema, no parser, nothing added to
your app.

55
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:26,264
Two things happen, and the order matters.

56
00:02:26,264 --> 00:02:30,043
First, anything sensitive is stripped
before a line touches disk.

57
00:02:30,043 --> 00:02:35,218
That ordering is the whole privacy story:
there is no unredacted copy downstream to
leak,

58
00:02:35,218 --> 00:02:36,671
because it never existed.

59
00:02:36,671 --> 00:02:38,939
Second, the text collapses into shapes.

60
00:02:38,939 --> 00:02:42,137
A hundred and seventy five lines become
nine templates.

61
00:02:42,137 --> 00:02:44,404
Everything above this counts templates,

62
00:02:44,404 --> 00:02:47,544
not lines, and that is what makes the rest
affordable.

63
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:52,737
And look at the counts, because the bug is
already sitting there.

64
00:02:52,737 --> 00:02:54,064
Twenty six orders received.

65
00:02:54,064 --> 00:02:57,552
Twenty four payments authorised, the two
missing ones are the declines.

66
00:02:57,552 --> 00:02:58,633
Twenty four completed.

67
00:02:58,633 --> 00:03:00,352
But only twenty one reserved stock.

68
00:03:00,352 --> 00:03:02,219
Twenty four minus twenty one is three.

69
00:03:02,219 --> 00:03:05,953
Three orders returned two hundred to a
paying customer and reserved nothing.

70
00:03:05,953 --> 00:03:07,181
Nobody set that check up.

71
00:03:07,181 --> 00:03:08,409
It falls out of counting.

72
00:03:11,009 --> 00:03:11,614
Stage two.

73
00:03:11,614 --> 00:03:14,458
Seven detectors decide what deserves
attention,

74
00:03:14,458 --> 00:03:17,787
and every one is arithmetic: counting,
ratios, medians.

75
00:03:17,787 --> 00:03:20,873
No model here at all, and that split is
deliberate.

76
00:03:20,873 --> 00:03:23,112
A threshold decides whether to speak.

77
00:03:23,112 --> 00:03:26,138
A model, later and only once, decides what
to say.

78
00:03:26,138 --> 00:03:29,466
Flip that and you are sending every log
line to an LLM,

79
00:03:29,466 --> 00:03:33,763
which nobody can afford or verify at four
hundred thousand lines a day.

80
00:03:35,763 --> 00:03:39,046
Stage three, and this is the bit no other
vendor computes.

81
00:03:39,046 --> 00:03:42,215
To judge a run you need to know what it
was meant to do,

82
00:03:42,215 --> 00:03:45,045
so Aegis mines that from the project's own
traces.

83
00:03:45,045 --> 00:03:48,497
Here is the flow spec: every step, and how
often it shows up.

84
00:03:48,497 --> 00:03:50,421
Order received, a hundred percent.

85
00:03:50,421 --> 00:03:51,553
Payment, ninety two.

86
00:03:51,553 --> 00:03:53,081
Stock reserved, eighty one.

87
00:03:53,081 --> 00:03:55,005
Now look at the badge on that row.

88
00:03:55,005 --> 00:03:55,911
Critical, human.

89
00:03:58,111 --> 00:03:59,891
Stage three also reads your code.

90
00:03:59,891 --> 00:04:02,911
Analyze parses the source with Python's
own syntax tree.

91
00:04:02,911 --> 00:04:04,259
No model reads your repo,

92
00:04:04,259 --> 00:04:07,926
because that would be unverifiable and
blow past any context window.

93
00:04:07,926 --> 00:04:10,946
From a hundred and forty four files:
twenty two ways in,

94
00:04:10,946 --> 00:04:13,696
twenty five dependencies, thirteen
unguarded calls.

95
00:04:13,696 --> 00:04:17,309
And every dependency is flagged red where
the code never guards it,

96
00:04:17,309 --> 00:04:19,574
which your logs could never have told you.

97
00:04:22,174 --> 00:04:22,993
So, that badge.

98
00:04:22,993 --> 00:04:26,267
Payment shows up in twenty four orders,
stock in twenty one.

99
00:04:26,267 --> 00:04:28,614
By frequency, payment looks more
essential.

100
00:04:28,614 --> 00:04:29,924
But what are we hunting?

101
00:04:29,924 --> 00:04:32,761
An order that charged the card and
reserved nothing.

102
00:04:32,761 --> 00:04:33,416
Payment ran.

103
00:04:33,416 --> 00:04:34,180
Stock did not.

104
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:35,381
Here is the principle,

105
00:04:35,381 --> 00:04:44,659
and it is the sharpest idea in the
product: the step that makes a run worth
having only shows up in the runs that
worked, which is exactly why counting can
never find it.

106
00:04:44,659 --> 00:04:47,169
So a human marks it, and the spec records
who.

107
00:04:49,769 --> 00:04:52,350
With that spec, every order gets a
verdict.

108
00:04:52,350 --> 00:04:53,430
Green did its job.

109
00:04:53,430 --> 00:04:57,931
Amber did the work but took six seconds
against a one second p ninety five.

110
00:04:57,931 --> 00:04:58,591
Red failed.

111
00:04:58,591 --> 00:05:02,192
And the purple cards are hollow: eight
eight four zero nine,

112
00:05:02,192 --> 00:05:03,812
four two two, four two six.

113
00:05:03,812 --> 00:05:05,312
Each one says it plainly.

114
00:05:05,312 --> 00:05:10,353
Completed cleanly, no errors, normal
teardown, and not one of its purpose steps
ran.

115
00:05:10,353 --> 00:05:13,834
Grep those numbers in the raw log and
check them yourself.

116
00:05:16,434 --> 00:05:18,676
Signals do not turn into forty alerts.

117
00:05:18,676 --> 00:05:22,038
The ones belonging to a single story
become one incident.

118
00:05:22,038 --> 00:05:22,923
Members listed,

119
00:05:22,923 --> 00:05:30,179
first one tagged ranked cause, and
underneath, why it ranked: earliest onset,
both sharing a trace, ranked on timing
alone.

120
00:05:30,179 --> 00:05:32,952
It shows its working instead of just
asserting.

121
00:05:32,952 --> 00:05:36,432
Then memory: a past incident at zero point
nine similarity,

122
00:05:36,432 --> 00:05:39,087
carrying the words precedent, not
conclusion.

123
00:05:41,287 --> 00:05:43,933
Stage four is the first time a model runs
at all,

124
00:05:43,933 --> 00:05:46,634
under three constraints that are enforced
in code.

125
00:05:46,634 --> 00:05:47,444
It is budgeted,

126
00:05:47,444 --> 00:05:52,035
because an outage is exactly when an
ungoverned reasoning layer would spend the
most.

127
00:05:52,035 --> 00:05:55,707
It is audited, and the record is written
before the call, not after.

128
00:05:55,707 --> 00:05:59,974
And it only ever sees redacted text,
because there is no other version to send.

129
00:06:02,374 --> 00:06:04,735
Hit Explain, and one governed call runs.

130
00:06:04,735 --> 00:06:07,331
The answer comes back tagged grounded,
high.

131
00:06:07,331 --> 00:06:09,928
Payment was declined for insufficient
funds,

132
00:06:09,928 --> 00:06:12,996
which made the checkout API record an
order failure.

133
00:06:12,996 --> 00:06:16,655
And underneath, the two log lines it rests
on, quoted exactly.

134
00:06:16,655 --> 00:06:18,366
That check is not decoration.

135
00:06:18,366 --> 00:06:22,497
Every citation gets matched against the
evidence the model was handed.

136
00:06:22,497 --> 00:06:25,035
Anything it made up is dropped and
counted,

137
00:06:25,035 --> 00:06:29,342
and if nothing survives, it reads
unverified and confidence drops to low.

138
00:06:29,342 --> 00:06:35,480
The research is blunt here: a good
explanation makes you trust wrong answers
just as much as right ones.

139
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:37,545
Only a checkable source fixes that.

140
00:06:40,145 --> 00:06:42,218
Stage five has a hard problem first.

141
00:06:42,218 --> 00:06:45,212
A log line has no pointer to the code that
wrote it.

142
00:06:45,212 --> 00:06:47,227
The two worlds share no identifier.

143
00:06:47,227 --> 00:06:52,237
So Aegis parses your source and matches
format strings back to the lines they
produced.

144
00:06:52,237 --> 00:06:54,943
That bridge is what lets it say reserve
dot py,

145
00:06:54,943 --> 00:06:58,398
line eighty nine, instead of something
went wrong somewhere.

146
00:06:58,398 --> 00:07:04,041
And it pulls double duty: the file it
mapped to becomes the only file a patch is
allowed to touch.

147
00:07:04,041 --> 00:07:05,711
Then write a test that fails.

148
00:07:05,711 --> 00:07:09,684
If it passes before the patch, the
diagnosis was wrong, and it stops.

149
00:07:12,484 --> 00:07:15,077
Here it is on a real service with a real
crash.

150
00:07:15,077 --> 00:07:17,062
Aegis mapped the evidence to source,

151
00:07:17,062 --> 00:07:20,041
wrote a reproducer, proved it failed,
drafted a patch.

152
00:07:20,041 --> 00:07:21,475
Then the gate rejected it.

153
00:07:21,475 --> 00:07:25,060
Two hunks removed the same line, so the
second could never apply.

154
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:25,502
Blocked.

155
00:07:25,502 --> 00:07:29,473
Aegis proposes only what it has proven,
and it could not prove this one.

156
00:07:32,273 --> 00:07:34,423
And when the trail goes cold, it says so.

157
00:07:34,423 --> 00:07:38,145
Research found twenty seven percent of
failures could not be diagnosed,

158
00:07:38,145 --> 00:07:40,242
because the evidence was never captured.

159
00:07:40,242 --> 00:07:42,444
No model recovers a log line nobody wrote.

160
00:07:42,444 --> 00:07:46,429
So this page tells you what to log next:
the gaps the analysis actually hit,

161
00:07:46,429 --> 00:07:48,578
each with the measurement that proves it.

162
00:07:50,978 --> 00:07:52,995
Stage six is the one nobody else has.

163
00:07:52,995 --> 00:07:57,410
A survey of the open source field turned
up nothing that asks whether a fix held,

164
00:07:57,410 --> 00:08:01,879
nothing that learns from recorded
outcomes, nothing that reports its own
accuracy.

165
00:08:01,879 --> 00:08:06,839
The one project with a real learning loop
marks that feature closed source in its
own docs.

166
00:08:06,839 --> 00:08:11,799
A vendor picking this as its moat is the
best evidence going of which part is worth
having.

167
00:08:14,399 --> 00:08:17,232
Every resolved incident gets archived
automatically,

168
00:08:17,232 --> 00:08:19,630
as a structured signature rather than
prose.

169
00:08:19,630 --> 00:08:22,354
Resolving is archiving, so nobody has to
remember.

170
00:08:22,354 --> 00:08:24,424
When something similar turns up later,

171
00:08:24,424 --> 00:08:28,510
memory hands the investigation its own
history before any hypothesis forms.

172
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:32,542
And a remembered wrong diagnosis is kept
just as carefully as a right one,

173
00:08:32,542 --> 00:08:36,792
because it stops the same bad answer
coming back with a precedent's authority.

174
00:08:39,192 --> 00:08:41,348
And then the measurement nobody does.

175
00:08:41,348 --> 00:08:45,076
Mark a fix as working and it freezes the
numbers to judge it by,

176
00:08:45,076 --> 00:08:46,882
then measures them again later.

177
00:08:46,882 --> 00:08:49,562
Two measures, because one does not
generalise.

178
00:08:49,562 --> 00:08:51,193
Latency answers a slow flow.

179
00:08:51,193 --> 00:08:52,184
But for an error,

180
00:08:52,184 --> 00:09:00,165
or a run of hollow checkouts, the timing
never moves, so the thing that always
works is whether the incident's own
templates fired again.

181
00:09:00,165 --> 00:09:00,456
Held,

182
00:09:00,456 --> 00:09:05,583
recurred with the count that proves it, or
too early, which says so instead of
guessing.

183
00:09:08,383 --> 00:09:09,974
Finally, what it does not do.

184
00:09:09,974 --> 00:09:10,851
It never merges,

185
00:09:10,851 --> 00:09:14,635
and that is not a setting: the capability
does not exist at any tier.

186
00:09:14,635 --> 00:09:16,994
It never edits a test to make a patch
pass.

187
00:09:16,994 --> 00:09:19,462
It never writes to the repo it is
diagnosing.

188
00:09:19,462 --> 00:09:21,601
One project's data is one file on disk.

189
00:09:21,601 --> 00:09:25,824
And the chatbot answers about the run in
front of you, not the whole archive.

190
00:09:25,824 --> 00:09:28,182
That is not built yet, and the docs say
so.

191
00:09:30,782 --> 00:09:31,838
One sentence to finish.

192
00:09:31,838 --> 00:09:36,978
Your dashboards will keep telling you
checkout returns two hundred in four
hundred and ninety five milliseconds,

193
00:09:36,978 --> 00:09:37,850
and they are right.

194
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:40,559
Aegis tells you three of those orders
never reserved stock,

195
00:09:40,559 --> 00:09:43,496
which line of code did it, and whether the
fix you shipped held.
